# VRHR Aggregator

Reads the most recent quarterly file (`Q<n>.<REGION>.<m>.<d>.<yy>.xlsx`) from each
regional OneDrive folder, aggregates it into a master
`ADFR.Region.Nation.Totals.New.xlsx` with a rolling monthly time series, LIVE
section, historical snapshots, and a Trends & Takeaways sheet.

---

## Files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `main.py` | Core aggregator — run this every refresh |
| `config.py` | All constants, column mappings, region definitions |
| `migrate.py` | One-time migration tool — see [Migration](#migration) below |

---

## Setup

### 1. Sync the source files from Teams/SharePoint

Before running the script, you need the regional VRHR folders available locally. In Teams or SharePoint:

1. Go to **ViiV Field Reimbursement Managers - 2026**
2. Click **Sync** (not Download) on the 2026 folder, or the regional subfolders you need — if you don't see Sync, check the `...` menu or right-click the folder.

**Sync vs. Download:**
- **Sync** mirrors the folder locally and keeps it updated automatically in the background as teammates edit source files. This is what the script expects — use this one.
- **Download** grabs a one-time static zip copy that never updates again. Don't use this.

Once synced, the folder appears locally at `C:\Users\<yourname>\GSK\ViiV Field Reimbursement Managers - 2026\...` and `config.py` finds it automatically — no path editing needed.

> Note: sync isn't instant. If you run the script right after a teammate edits a file, give OneDrive a minute to catch up before running.

### 2. Install Python and dependencies

```
python -m venv venv
.\venv\Scripts\python.exe -m pip install openpyxl
```
Python 3.10+ recommended.

`config.py` auto-detects `BASE_2026`, `OUTPUT`, and `OLD_FILE` from your home directory once the folder above is synced — no changes needed. If it can't find the folder (e.g. your OneDrive folder isn't named `GSK`, or the sync folder name has changed), it raises a clear error telling you which environment variable to set instead (`VRHR_BASE_2026`, `VRHR_OUTPUT`, `VRHR_OLD_FILE`).

---

## Usage

Normal refresh:
```
python main.py
```

Run this monthly (or as often as you want a refresh). It's safe to run multiple
times within the same month — it detects that and just refreshes the LIVE data in
place rather than duplicating a row or freezing a snapshot early.

---

## How It Works

Each run of `main.py`:

1. **Read regional files.** For each of the 6 regions, finds the most recently
   dated quarterly file (`Q<n>.<REGION>.<m>.<d>.<yy>.xlsx`) — quarter number isn't
   hardcoded, so Q4 gets picked up automatically once it exists, no code changes
   needed. Within that file:
   - Barrier booleans (output cols 3-8) and Pathway-mapped-onward (cols 24-40) are
     read positionally off the main `"...vrhr..."` sheet's row 3 (TOTALS row) — this
     part of the layout hasn't moved.
   - Customer Engagement Phase fields (cols 19-23) are read from the same main sheet,
     but matched by **header text**, not position (see [Header-Text Lookup](#header-text-lookup) below).
   - Barrier Tracking fields (cols 9-18) are read from a separate `"...Barrier
     Movement..."` sheet, also matched by header text.
   - Campus count is counted dynamically from the file's data rows, falling back to
     `REGION_ACCOUNTS` in config only if that fails.

2. **Open or create the output file.** If it doesn't exist yet, creates a fresh
   workbook with headers and column widths. If it exists, loads it and (only if the
   time series is completely empty) backfills time-series rows from any existing
   snapshot blocks.

3. **Write the current month into the time series**, rotating the oldest row out
   once 12 months are filled. Matches an existing row by **year and month together**,
   not just month name — see [Year-Aware Matching](#year-aware-matching).

4. **Freeze a snapshot if the month changed.** Compares the LIVE section's last
   refresh date to today by year+month (not just month name — see below) — if it's a
   new month, the current LIVE section is frozen into a dated snapshot block before
   being overwritten. Snapshots are capped at 11, oldest trimmed automatically.

5. **Write the new LIVE section** — raw counts + percentage sub-sections, one row
   per region plus a NATION summary row. LIVE floats dynamically to sit one blank row
   below the last filled time-series row.

6. **Build the Trends & Takeaways sheet** from the time series and LIVE pct section.

7. **Apply column widths and formatting**, then save. Column widths only get
   backfilled where missing — manual resizing in Excel is preserved run to run (see
   [Column Widths](#column-widths) below).

---

## Sheet Structure — Territory ADFR Totals

```
Row 1           Title
Row 3           Time series header (Months, Total Barriers, col headers)
Rows 4-15       Time series (one row per month, max 12, strictly chronological)
[blank row]
LIVE row        "LIVE - refreshed M.DD.YY" (yellow fill)
                Raw counts section (section header + col header + 6 regions + NATION)
                [gap row]
                % section (section header + col header + 6 regions + NATION)
[gap row]
Snapshots       Historical frozen snapshots (yellow date row + raw + pct blocks)
                Max 11 snapshots kept, oldest trimmed automatically
```

### Column layout (40 columns)

| Cols | Section | Source |
|---|---|---|
| 1-2 | Region, Total Accounts | — |
| 3-8 | Implementation Barriers | Main sheet, positional |
| 9-18 | Barrier Tracking (Barrier 1/2 Category, Subcategory, Start/Close Date, Stage) | **Barrier Movement sheet**, header-text lookup |
| 19-23 | Customer Engagement Phase | Main sheet, header-text lookup |
| 24-30 | Mapping the Pathway | Main sheet, positional |
| 31-34 | Buy and Bill Pathway | Main sheet, positional |
| 35-40 | Specialty Pharmacy Pathway | Main sheet, positional |

---

## Sheet Structure — Trends & Takeaways

- **Title + subtitle** — refresh timestamp, total campuses, current total barriers
- **Key Metrics** — 5 stat cards: Total Barriers, Top Barrier Type, Pathway Mapped, Buy & Bill, SP
- **Barrier Trends** — table with Month, Total Barriers, MoM Change, 6 barrier type %. Green/red MoM coloring. Chart at column V (2+ months required)
- **Barrier Breakdown** — heatmap per region: red >60%, yellow 30–60%, green <30%
- **Pathway & Access** — read directly from the LIVE pct section (Pathway Mapped col 24, Buy & Bill cols 31–34, SP cols 37–40)

---

## Regions

| Region | Campus count |
|---|---|
| West Coast | Dynamic — counted from file each run |
| Central | Dynamic |
| Great Lakes | Dynamic |
| NorthEast | Dynamic |
| SouthEast | Dynamic |
| MidAtlantic | Dynamic |
| NATION | Sum / mode of all regions (see below) |

Campus counts are read live from each region's quarterly file every run
(`count_campus_rows`). The `REGION_ACCOUNTS` values in `config.py` are only a
fallback used if a file's campus count can't be determined.

**NATION row logic:** numeric columns (`NATION_SUM_COLS`) are summed across regions.
Two text columns — "Independent decision making..." (col 19) and "Level of ViiV
Connect user" (col 23) — are categorical, not numeric, so NATION instead shows
whichever answer is **most common** across regions (`NATION_MODE_COLS`), not a sum.

---

## Header-Text Lookup

Barrier Tracking (cols 9-18) and Customer Engagement Phase (cols 19-23) are **not**
read by fixed column position. The source layout for this region of the file has
already shifted once — a Subcategory column was inserted and "Prioritized Barrier
3" was dropped when the separate Barrier Movement sheet was introduced — so a fixed
position silently breaks the moment a column gets inserted, removed, or reordered
again.

Instead, `BARRIER_MOVEMENT_FIELDS` and `CEP_FIELDS` in `config.py` each list, per
output column, one or more candidate header strings to search for in row 6 of the
relevant sheet. The first candidate that matches wins. If none match, the script
prints a warning naming the output column and the candidates it tried, and leaves
that field blank rather than silently pulling the wrong data.

If a region's file has no sheet matching `"barrier movement"` at all (e.g. it
hasn't been updated to the new format yet), cols 9-18 are left blank with a warning
— everything else in that region's row still populates normally.

---

## Year-Aware Matching

Two places compare "is this the same month as before" — both do it by **(year,
month)**, not month name alone:

- **Time series row matching** (`update_time_series`): without year-awareness, a
  new January would match an *existing* January row from a prior year (still sitting
  in the 12-row time-series block) by name and silently overwrite it in place instead
  of rotating a new row in — scrambling the chronological order and corrupting the
  Trends & Takeaways month-over-month chart.
- **Snapshot rollover detection** (`detect_live_month` / `main`): without
  year-awareness, if a full year+ passes with no runs and the next run happens to
  land on the same calendar month as the last one, the script would think "same
  month, just refresh" and silently overwrite the old LIVE data with **no snapshot
  ever taken** — a full year of data lost with no trace.

Both are covered by tests simulating multi-year runs; see the codebase's git history
/ prior conversation for the specific reproductions if this ever needs re-verifying.

---

## Column Widths

`apply_column_widths(ws, force=...)`:
- **`force=True`** (first run only, brand-new file): sets the full intended layout.
- **`force=False`** (every later run): only fills in a width for a column that has
  **no width recorded at all** — never overwrites a width that's already present.
  This means manual resizing in Excel survives future runs, while still backfilling
  any column that's missing a width entirely (e.g. if the layout grows and a new
  column is added).

---

## Migration

`migrate.py` is a **one-time** tool for moving an *existing* output file's
historical time-series data into a new column layout after `config.py`'s column
structure changes (e.g. when Barrier Tracking grew from 11 to 15 columns). It is
**not** part of the normal refresh flow and does not run automatically.

It touches **only** the top time-series table (row 3 header + month rows) — it does
**not** touch the LIVE section (which gets fully rewritten by `main.py` on its next
run anyway) or any snapshot blocks.

Usage — pass the path to your **existing** output file as an argument:
```
python migrate.py "C:\Users\<you>\GSK\ViiV Field Reimbursement Managers - 2026\ADFR.Region.Nation.Totals.New.xlsx"
```

It never overwrites your original — it writes a new file alongside it with
`.MIGRATED.xlsx` appended to the name. Open the migrated copy, confirm it looks
right, then rename it into place (or rename your original to something like
`...OLD.xlsx` first, then drop the `.MIGRATED` suffix from the new one) before
running `main.py` again.

Only re-run `migrate.py` if `config.py`'s column layout changes again in a way that
shifts existing columns — it is not something you run every month.

---

## Key Architecture Decisions

**Dynamic LIVE placement**
`get_live_offsets(ws)` is the single source of truth for all LIVE section row numbers.
Derives every row position from `get_live_base(ws)`, which scans for the last filled
month. Change LIVE layout in one place only.

**Dynamic snapshot start**
`get_snap_start(ws)` derives from `get_live_offsets`, so snapshot positions
automatically adjust as months accumulate through the year.

**No hardcoded row numbers**
`TS_END_ROW = TS_START_ROW + 11` in config (12 months max). Everything else derived.

**Dynamic file discovery**
`find_sprint_file` picks the most recent quarterly file by parsing the date out of
the filename itself (`m.d.yy` tokens), falling back to file modified time only if
the filename can't be parsed. No quarter number is hardcoded — Q4 files are picked
up automatically once they exist.

**Header-text lookup for the volatile columns**
See [Header-Text Lookup](#header-text-lookup) above — cols 9-23 are matched by
header wording, not position, since that region of the source layout has already
shifted once.

**Year-aware month comparisons**
See [Year-Aware Matching](#year-aware-matching) above.

**Column width preservation**
See [Column Widths](#column-widths) above.

**Pathway & Access data**
Read directly from the Territory sheet's LIVE pct section rather than recomputed,
ensuring Trends & Takeaways always matches the Territory sheet.

---

## Config Reference

| Setting | Description |
|---|---|
| `BASE_2026` | Root path to the 2026 OneDrive folder — auto-detected from home directory, override with `VRHR_BASE_2026` |
| `OUTPUT` | Path to `ADFR.Region.Nation.Totals.New.xlsx` — override with `VRHR_OUTPUT` |
| `OLD_FILE` | Path to the original `ADFR.Region.Nation.Totals.xlsx` — override with `VRHR_OLD_FILE`. Currently unused by `main.py`; kept for legacy/migration reference |
| `REGIONS` | List of (label, folder) tuples |
| `REGION_ACCOUNTS` | Fallback campus counts per region, used only if dynamic counting fails |
| `NATION_TOTAL` | Auto-derived as sum of `REGION_ACCOUNTS` at runtime |
| `MAIN_SHEET_KEYWORD` | `"vrhr"` — keyword used to find the main quarterly sheet |
| `BARRIER_MOVEMENT_SHEET_KEYWORD` | `"barrier movement"` — keyword used to find the Barrier Movement sheet |
| `SOURCE_HEADER_ROW` | Row 6 — header row on both source sheets, used for header-text lookup |
| `SPRINT_COLS` | `{output_col: source_col}` for the positional (unchanged) parts of the layout: barrier booleans and Pathway-onward |
| `BARRIER_MOVEMENT_FIELDS` | `{output_col: [candidate header strings]}` for cols 9-18, matched on the Barrier Movement sheet |
| `CEP_FIELDS` | `{output_col: [candidate header strings]}` for cols 19-23, matched on the main sheet |
| `NATION_SUM_COLS` | Output cols summed for the NATION row / time series % |
| `NATION_MODE_COLS` | Output cols (19, 23) where NATION shows the most-common value instead of a sum |
| `PCT_COLS` | Output cols shown as percentages in the LIVE % section |
| `TS_HEADER_ROW` | Row 3 — time series header |
| `TS_START_ROW` | Row 4 — first monthly data row |
| `TS_END_ROW` | Row 15 — last monthly data row (derived, max 12 months) |
| `SECTION_HEADERS` | (start_col, end_col, label, hex_color) for each section |
| `COL_HEADERS` | `{col: header_text}` for every output column (1-40) |
| `YELLOW` | Hex color for LIVE row and snapshot date rows |
| `NATION_FILL` | Hex color for NATION row background |

---

## Updating for a New Year

1. Update `BASE_2026` path in `config.py` (or the `VRHR_BASE_2026` env var) to the new year folder
2. Update `OUTPUT` path
3. Delete or archive the previous year's output file
4. Run `main.py` fresh — it will start a new time series

---

## Troubleshooting

**"No file found in [folder]"**
Check that the regional folder exists and contains a file matching `Q<n>.<REGION>.<m>.<d>.<yy>.xlsx`.

**"Could not read row 3 on main sheet"**
Quarterly file found but no sheet matching `"vrhr"` exists. Check the sheet name inside the file.

**"No 'barrier movement' sheet found — barrier tracking columns (9-18) will be blank"**
That region's file doesn't have a sheet matching `"barrier movement"` in its name yet.
Everything else (barrier booleans, CEP fields, Pathway-onward) still populates
normally — only cols 9-18 are blank for that region until the file is updated.

**"Could not find header for output col X (tried: [...]) — leaving blank"**
A header-text lookup (cols 9-23) didn't find a match on the expected sheet. Usually
means the source file's column header wording changed. Check the candidate strings
listed in the warning against the actual header row (row 6) of that sheet, and
update `BARRIER_MOVEMENT_FIELDS` / `CEP_FIELDS` in `config.py` if the wording has
genuinely changed.

**MergedCell error**
Should not occur. `unmerge_row` is called before any write to time series rows.

**LIVE section in wrong position**
Delete the output file and rerun `main.py` from scratch.

**Snapshot count growing too large**
`_trim_snapshots` keeps max 11 automatically. No action needed.

**Time series rows look out of order, or a snapshot seems to be missing after a long gap**
Should not happen — see [Year-Aware Matching](#year-aware-matching). If it does,
it likely means the LIVE label's date (`LIVE - refreshed M.DD.YY`) or a time-series
row's date got hand-edited into an unparseable format. Check that dates in column A
still match the expected `Month (MM/DD/YY)` / `LIVE - refreshed M.D.YY` patterns.

**`FileNotFoundError: BASE_2026 not found`**
Your OneDrive sync folder isn't named `GSK` under your home directory, the sync
folder name has changed, or you haven't synced the folder yet (see Setup above).
Set the `VRHR_BASE_2026` environment variable to your actual local path instead of
editing `config.py`.